WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
Sixteen solo skippers have gathered in Les Sables d'Olonne to begin final preparations for the

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:12.240
2026 Golden Globe Race, the only round-the-world event sailed in 1960s Arab boats without modern electronics

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:18.780
or outside assistance. This is Offshore North, Episode 2. Scott Shawyer is working to become the first

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:26.080
Canadian to finish the Vendée Globe alone and non-stop in 2028. On the Canadian boat, as of the campaign's

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:33.620
most recent public update on 17 August, 2026, Emiraphore's exact position after its 8-9 August

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:39.680
departure from Collingwood remains unconfirmed in this week's sources. Canada Ocean Racing checked

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:45.780
its own website, news section, and YouTube channel this week, and the newest item on all three is a 17

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:51.080
August profile of the campaign's motivation. That leaves the open question of the boat's current

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:56.780
location still open, which matters because every solo mile on this hull now counts toward both

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:03.100
Globe Series points and the published Grade 2 Qualifier requirement for 2028. The boat's history

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:09.120
already shows what those miles can deliver. It won the Ocean Race 2023 under Charlie N. Wright,

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:16.300
took Thirrd in the last Vendée Globe with Sebastian Simon despite a broken foil and set a solo 24-hour

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:23.340
speed record before Scott Shawyer took it over in early 2025. Now, the fleet. Sixteen solo skippers

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:28.660
are now assembled in Lesablet de Lone, two weeks before the start of the Mac and Tire Golden Globe Race

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:35.980
2026. Each must sail a production boat built before 1988 and navigate only with the equipment available

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:42.900
in the original 1968 race. So, Saxton, Papercharts, and Barometer readings replace any satellite systems.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:48.820
After a prologue won by Damien Gu, the fleet moves into its final preparation for a nonstop,

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.780
unassisted circumnavigation expected to last roughly eight months.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:57.260
Teams are gathering in Newport, Rhode Island ahead of the Ocean Race Atlantic.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:01.460
The event forms the next stage of the Ocean Race's two-year global calendar.

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:05.500
It will be sailed by crews that include the DMG Mori sailing team.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.320
That team's campaign was profiled this week in an official video release from the organizers.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:15.740
The Ocean Race Atlantic continues the series' emphasis on crewed offshore racing across the

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:21.480
North Atlantic. This provides competitive miles and media exposure for teams preparing longer campaigns.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:27.060
Newport's selection as a stopover leverages the ports established infrastructure for high-performance

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:30.880
yacht preparation and its history of hosting major offshore events.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:37.400
Alexia Barrier's Idex Sport team has begun its Root-Durum 2026 preparation program.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:43.460
The solo trans-Atlantic race starts one November from St. Mallow. Barrier's campaign is one of the

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:48.780
early public efforts to secure a birth in the IM-OCA class for the 13th edition of the event.

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:52.880
The team's announcement frames the work as the formal beginning of an adventure.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:58.160
That adventure will see the boat and skipper tested across the Atlantic in the largest mixed class

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:03.100
offshore start in the sport. Root-Durum participation also offers barrier and

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:07.900
opportunity to gain the solo offshore experience required for future qualification pathways.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:11.940
The team is therefore using this program to build both the boat's readiness

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:16.460
and the skipper's solo miles ahead of the start. That brings us to plain sailing.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:21.960
The Golden Globe race and the Vende Globe both send one person alone around the world without stopping

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:27.940
yet they test entirely different skills. The Golden Globe uses only boats designed before 1988

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:33.760
and forbids GPS, satellite phones, or weather routing computers. So skippers navigate with a

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:39.720
sextant and paper charts the way the original 1968 competitors did. The Vende Globe by contrast

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:46.140
is sailed in current generation foiling IM-OCA's that can exceed 40 knots and carry the full sweet

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:50.720
of modern satellite systems. Because the boats are slower and the information poorer,

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:55.380
a Golden Globe skipper's daily decisions rest on what can be seen from the deck and what the

00:03:55.620 --> 00:04:01.200
and paper weather maps suggest. That constraint turns every cloud build up and wind shift into a

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:06.280
high stakes calculation made without the safety net of real-time forecasts or outside assistance.

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:11.780
A skipper may spend weeks without speaking to another human, rationing food, water, and energy

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:17.660
while the boat moves at perhaps one third the speed of an IM-OCA. The absence of electronic

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:22.760
autopilots and routing software also means the sailor must steer and trim continuously during

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:27.360
daylight hours and accept whatever weather arrives rather than threading between systems.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:33.520
The result is a race whose difficulty lies less in raw speed than in sustained self-reliance over

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:39.100
roughly eight months at sea. A listener can picture it as the difference between driving a modern rally

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:45.460
car with live telemetry and driving a 1960s vehicle across the same continents using only a road

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:51.120
atlas and a compass. The Golden Globe therefore measures endurance of isolation and judgment under

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:56.600
deliberately limited tools while the Vende Globe measures the ability to extract maximum performance

00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:02.540
from the most advanced offshore machines ever built. Both formats reward the same core trait,

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:07.960
making correct decisions when no one else can help, but they remove different variables to isolate

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:14.080
that trait. One removes technology, the other removes company. A listener who finishes this explanation

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:20.140
can state that the Golden Globe's eight-month duration and pre-1988 boats create a test of pure

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:26.480
self-reliance that modern IAMOCA racing deliberately replaces with speed and connectivity and the

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:33.920
countdown. 811 days remain until the Vende Globe start on 12 November 2028. The campaign's

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:40.260
Lex Concrete Milestone is the Root Do Room, scheduled to start 1 November 2026 from St. Mallow.

